      ],
      "why": "Full pipelined has one register per stage, so area scales with N. It only made the front once (1273), so I give it a smaller share and a tighter, lower-N range (10..14) with minimal guards to find its absolute best corner. If even its best corner can't beat pipelined_m's 959, it contributes little to hypervolume, but a few evals confirm the boundary."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >=1 MSPS (so pipelined/pipelined_m are the right families, not iterative/unrolled_k) and max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Area is the primary objective. From fleet facts, pipelined_m already has a front member at 959 luts+ffs while full pipelined's best is 1273. The area lever in pipelined_m is m (registers every m stages): larger m means fewer FFs but tighter per-stage timing. I split the 10-eval budget 50/50: 5 evals on pipelined_m exploring the m/n_iter/data_width/guard trade-off to push the area down and map the accuracy-vs-area curve, and 5 evals on pipelined at low N to confirm its best corner. Accuracy is controlled by n_iter and data_width; with n_iter>=12 and data_width>=14, 2^-10 is comfortably met even with truncation and zero guards, so I keep guards minimal to save area. The hypervolume reward comes from covering the trade-off: pipelined_m with varying m and n_iter should trace a curve from low-area/low-accuracy to higher-area/higher-accuracy, which is exactly what the front needs."
}

